# Product surface — no vendor guts

On anything a **user, visitor, agent customer, or Run log** sees, AFT is just AFT.

**Never say** on platform UI, website (except intentional `/vs/` competitor pages), CLI user copy, job `line`/`reason`/log tails, API error messages, MCP tool descriptions, or agent-facing advice:

- Wrangler, OpenNext, `@opennextjs/*`, workers.dev
- Cloudflare / Workers / Pages / R2 / D1 / Durable Objects (as *our* stack)
- GHA, GitHub Actions, Sandbox SDK, “edge”, account IDs

**Do say:** build, deploy, live URL, Next.js, static, installing, failed — product verbs and frameworks the *user’s* repo already claims.

## Where this applies

| Surface | Rule |
| --- | --- |
| Run job phases / streamed logs / fail reasons | Product language only; redact vendor tokens before PATCH |
| `/v1/*` errors and check plan fields shown to clients | No vendor command lines |
| `www/` product docs, MCP public docs | No OpenNext/Wrangler how-to |
| CLI step titles and thrown errors | “Building…”, “Deploy failed” — not `wrangler deploy` |
| Ops / internal STRATEGY / engineer README | OK to name vendors |

## Exceptions

- **`/vs/cloudflare-drop/`** (and similar): competitor name is the point.
- **Privacy / legal** subprocessors list: real legal names stay.
- **Code comments, scripts, wrangler.jsonc, internal ops**: engineer surface — fine.

## Default when unsure

If Rohit (or any visitor) could read it on aft.page or in a Run log, strip the vendor word. Canonical intent: STRATEGY “Never expose Cloudflare complexity to the user.”
